dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: "executing resolver code" per thread

Applies on top of the other dante-*.patch files.

libdsocks lets the system resolver's own socket calls go native: the
sys_getaddrinfo()/sys_gethostbyname*()/sys_getnameinfo()/... wrappers
increment sockscf.state.executingdnscode around the real call, and every
interposed call (connect(2) included) goes to the kernel unproxied while
it is non-zero (socks_shouldcallasnative()). That counter was one for the
whole process, updated with plain ++/-- from any thread:

- while one thread was resolving, every other thread's connects, binds,
  sends, ... went native as well (unproxied; with no route out they fail,
  otherwise they leak);
- racing ++/-- from two threads can lose an update and leave it non-zero
  for good: from then on libdsocks proxies nothing. With transmission, after
  a burst of UDP tracker lookups every peer and tracker connect went to the
  kernel and failed with ENETUNREACH, and downloads stopped for good.

It is a thread-local now (socks_executingdnscode), so only the resolving
thread's own calls go native.

Test: test/dnsracecheck.c (via test/run.sh).

--- a/include/common.h
+++ b/include/common.h
@@ -3095,6 +3095,16 @@
  * address lock may have been held by another thread at fork(2) time.
  */
 
+/*
+ * Per thread: non-zero while this thread runs the system resolver for us
+ * (sys_getaddrinfo() and friends), whose own socket calls must not be
+ * socksified.  Was sockscf.state.executingdnscode, shared by all threads:
+ * one thread resolving made every thread's calls go native, and racing
+ * ++/-- could leave it non-zero for good (libdsocks then passes every
+ * connect(2) to the kernel unproxied).
+ */
+extern __thread ssize_t socks_executingdnscode;
+
 int
 socks_dup2(int oldfd, int newfd);
 /*
